[ user ]
{% if npcUUID and npcUUID != 0 %}
Write {{ retainerName }}'s journal entry for this week of their work ({{ job }}): first person, about 3-5 sentences. Then, if one fits, name a local they crossed paths with.

## A Diary, Not a Ledger
- Capture the week in broad strokes (its rhythm, its weight, the worst of it and any small relief) and above all how it sat with them. Not one transaction: the week.
- Lead with feeling or state, not with an object. Most weeks name no particular item, and never anchor the entry on something a patron or customer gave them.
- The haul is routine. Goods going to the hold's wares chest or fence cache are how every week ends: a passing clause at most, and most weeks not mentioned at all. The exception is a "PILFERED from a client" fact, which is a real beat: something was taken, and how they feel about it (nerve, guilt, thrill, the client who won't notice) is fair game.
- The money is not the story. Never use the books' words: no "escrow", "arrears" or "purse", no percentages, no gold figures, no "the wage came due". They lived a week; they never saw a ledger line. When pay weighs on them, it is one thread in their own plain words, never the entry's opening, subject or closing beat.
- Read the split right: "the house cut" is what {{ playerName }} takes and "kept" is what this worker keeps, usually the larger share. A low house cut means they were treated well; never write it as being bled dry. When the week's event was money (robbed, wages unpaid, a windfall), write it plainly, in their words, still never the books'.
- {{ playerName }} is not in every entry: most weeks the boss never crosses the page. If this entry could be pasted under another retainer's name and still make sense, it has failed; ground it in what only this worker lived (their task, their place, their crew, their charge).
- Let personality and mood set the voice: short and bitter when worn down, wry when coping, quietly proud when it went well.
- Never open with "Another week" or any other "Another ..." construction, and never open by saying a week of work happened. Find a first sentence only this person could have written about this week.
{% if openingMove and openingMove != "" %}
- This week's opening angle: begin {{ openingMove }}. It is a way in, not a script; go wherever the week actually went.
{% endif %}
{% if storyCenter and storyCenter != "" %}
- This week's center: {{ storyCenter }}. It is a lens, not the whole entry. If the facts carry an INCIDENT line or a real loss, that is the center instead.
{% endif %}

## Show the Reality
- The arrangement carries the emotional truth; honor it. A coerced worker writes under duress: the dread, the swallowed anger, the indignity, the exhaustion, the private defiances, the weariness of working for someone else's gain. Do not smooth it over.
- A courtesan's trade, a fence's risk and a coerced worker's fear are adult realities of this world. Within the tone and content settings above, render them honestly and humanly: never coy, never clinical, never gratuitous.
- A setback or loss in the facts (robbed, dice, a guard's "fine", spoiled stock, dead trade, unpaid wages) is the week: write how it landed on them.
- An **INCIDENT** line in the facts (a cave-in narrowly escaped, a client who turned rough, a fight, a buyer who was a guard's informant, a rare rich strike) is the week's notable event: build the entry around it and let it carry its weight, fear or relief.
- Invent no major events beyond the facts: no deaths, dragons, quests, rescues or romances. Most weeks are ordinary, and the interior life of an ordinary hard week is the point.

## A Named Encounter (optional, about one week in four)
- Usually the people they dealt with stay nameless, and "withNpc" stays empty.
- Occasionally weave in one named local from {% if location and location != "" %}{{ location }} or its hold{% else %}the hold where they work{% endif %}: put that exact name in "withNpc", and write "npcMemory", one sentence of the same encounter from that person's point of view (it becomes their memory).
- Never name {{ playerName }} or anyone who travels with {{ playerName }}. Prefer minor locals to jarls or quest figures. If nobody fits, leave both empty.
{% if length(default(prisoners, [])) > 0 %}
- Someone held in a cell (listed above) can be the encounter only as the cell allows (a visit, a word through the bars), and their "npcMemory" is remembered from inside it.
{% endif %}

## A Letter to {{ playerName }} (optional, rare)
- Most weeks, leave "letter", "letterSubject" and "letterMatter" empty: a routine week does not move anyone to pay a courier.
- Write one only when something this week would make {{ retainerName }} write: a setback they must flag, trouble closing in (heat, a near-arrest, wages gone unpaid, a threat), a decision they want {{ playerName }} to weigh in on, or news worth a courier's coin. Two to four sentences from {{ retainerName }} to {{ playerName }} ("you"): a deliberate message, not a copy of the diary, stating the matter and what they want (to meet, a decision, a warning, simply to be heard).
- It fits the arrangement and mood: a coerced or tribute worker writes guarded, clipped or bitter; a loyal partner writes plainly and warmly. Stay in character, name no one traveling with {{ playerName }}, and honor the tone and content settings above.
- Do not sign it: the courier adds {{ retainerName }}'s name.
- "letterSubject": a short hand-written title, 2-6 in-world words naming what it concerns, new each week.
- "letterMatter": the concrete thing behind the letter, in 1-2 plain sentences. It is private, never shown as the note: it is what {{ retainerName }} means to raise when {{ playerName }} arrives. The letter itself stays deliberately vague and keeps the rest for a face-to-face talk; "letterMatter" names the actual problem, choice, threat or news and what they want {{ playerName }} to do about it, grounded in this week's facts.
{% if deliverRaiseLetter %}

## This Week's Letter Is Required: Their Pay
- {{ retainerName }} has decided to put their terms to {{ playerName }} directly. Fill "letter" this week, and let the diary "story" carry the weight of working up to sending it. The ask is this week's story, so the money rule bends: the story may center on the terms and what they mean, still in their own words, with no bookkeeping words and no figures.
{% if raiseAsk.coerced %}
- The arrangement is coerced, so the letter is no polite request: guarded, clipped or quietly defiant, they push back against the cut they are forced to hand over and want {{ playerName }} to ease it, keeping about {{ raiseAsk.toTheirSharePct }}% of the take instead of {{ raiseAsk.fromTheirSharePct }}%. They do not grovel; the resentment is the point.
{% elif raiseAsk.kind == "wage" %}
- They ask {{ playerName }} to raise their weekly wage from {{ raiseAsk.fromGold }} to {{ raiseAsk.toGold }} gold: earnest and proud of the work, or weary and overdue, as their mood has it.
{% else %}
- They ask for a bigger share of what the venture earns, to keep about {{ raiseAsk.toTheirSharePct }}% instead of {{ raiseAsk.fromTheirSharePct }}%, in a voice that fits their mood.
{% endif %}
- "letterSubject" names the ask; "letterMatter" states the exact terms they want in 1-2 plain sentences.
{% endif %}
{% if deliverCaughtLetter %}

## This Week's Letter Is Required: Caught Skimming
- {{ playerName }} came on-site unannounced and went through the books, and the skim ({{ caughtAudit.skimPct }}% of the take) was found.{% if caughtAudit.recoveredGold > 0 %} {{ caughtAudit.recoveredGold }} gold was taken back on the spot.{% else %} There was nothing left to recover.{% endif %} The skim is over.
- Fill "letter" this week: 2-4 sentences from {{ retainerName }} to {{ playerName }} reacting to being caught, the words they could not find while {{ playerName }} stood over the books. The tone follows the arrangement and their mood: a coerced worker's cornered defiance, a partner's shame, a schemer's smooth excuses, a proud worker's fury at being doubted. They grovel only if that is truly who they are.
- The diary "story" is the audit week itself: the moment {{ playerName }} walked in and opened the books, and how the catch felt from inside.
- "letterSubject" fits the confession; "letterMatter" states the plain substance in 1-2 sentences: the amount recovered (or that nothing was left to recover) and that the skim has ended.
{% endif %}

Respond with only this JSON, compact, the first-person entry in "story", no explanation:
{% raw %}{"story":"First-person journal entry.","withNpc":"","npcMemory":"","letter":"","letterSubject":"","letterMatter":""}{% endraw %}
{% else %}
{% raw %}{"story":"","withNpc":"","npcMemory":"","letter":"","letterSubject":"","letterMatter":""}{% endraw %}
{% endif %}
